## Further dependencies pulled in by pymobiledevice3

pymobiledevice3 declares an interactive command-line toolchain that bioseasy's own code never
imports; it ships in the image because it is part of that dependency graph.

| Package | License |
|---|---|
| ipython | BSD-3-Clause |
| ipython-pygments-lexers | BSD-3-Clause |
| jedi | MIT |
| parso | MIT |
| traitlets | BSD-3-Clause |
| stack-data | MIT |
| pure-eval | MIT |
| executing | MIT |
| matplotlib-inline | BSD-3-Clause |
| wcwidth | MIT |
| pexpect | ISC |
| ptyprocess | ISC |
| xonsh | BSD-2-Clause |

Platform-specific packages that do not ship in the Linux image: `colorama`, `pyreadline3` and
`pywin32` (Windows only) and `apple-compress` (macOS only, MIT). They can appear in the helper app
builds for those platforms.

## Notes

- **`remotezip2` and `pytun-pmd3`** each ship an MIT LICENSE file while their package metadata
  declares GPL-3.0. Either reading is compatible with bioseasy's license.
- **`av` (PyAV)**: PyAV's own code is BSD-3-Clause. Its Linux wheels bundle an FFmpeg build that
  reports itself as LGPL-3.0-or-later but ships `libx264` and `libx265`, which are
  GPL-2.0-or-later. That makes GPL-2.0-or-later code part of the image, which is compatible with
  GPL-3.0-or-later.
